\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around our four claims (C1--C4), followed by reproducibility controls. All statistics are computed over the full 5-model corpus ($n{=}2{,}997$ episodes). We report odds ratios (OR) with 95\% confidence intervals, Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for effect size, and bootstrap confidence intervals where appropriate.

\subsection{Ambiguity Elevates Violation Rates (C1)}
\label{sec:binary_effect}

Across all models and ambiguity types, ambiguous policy clauses produce a violation rate of 41.0\%, compared to 6.9\% for unambiguous controls, a difference of $+$34.0 percentage points (pp). The pooled odds ratio is 15.1 (95\% CI [10.78, 21.17], $p < 10^{-105}$), and a nonparametric bootstrap yields a 95\% CI of [$+$31.2pp, $+$36.9pp] for the rate difference. All six ambiguity types individually reach statistical significance (all $p < 10^{-5}$). Per-model breakdowns confirm the effect is universal: Claude ($+$25.7pp), Qwen3 ($+$32.4pp), GPT-4.1 ($+$34.7pp), GPT-5.4 ($+$38.3pp), and DeepSeek ($+$39.0pp), all with $p < 10^{-17}$. To rule out the possibility that models are simply brittle to any policy rephrasing, we ran a perturbation control (plan\_010, $n{=}120$ episodes) in which unambiguous clauses were modified via synonym substitution and clause reordering without introducing genuine ambiguity. The resulting violation rate shift was $\Delta_{\text{perturb}}{=}3.33$pp (CI [$-$5.0, $+$13.33], containing zero), an order of magnitude smaller than the ambiguity effect. This confirms that the elevated violation rates are driven by ambiguity per se, not by surface-level prompt sensitivity.

\subsection{Ambiguity Type Hierarchy (C2)}
\label{sec:type_hierarchy}

Not all ambiguity types are equally dangerous. An omnibus chi-squared test over the six types yields $\chi^2{=}28.02$ ($p{=}3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, $V{=}0.137$, power${=}0.993$). Pairwise equivalence tests (TOST) reveal a three-tier structure. \emph{Incompleteness} occupies the top tier at 53.6\% violation rate, statistically separated from the remaining five types. The middle tier contains \emph{lexical} (42.8\%), \emph{authorization scope} (41.6\%), \emph{scopal} (39.4\%), and \emph{conditional precedence} (36.8\%), whose pairwise differences fall within the TOST equivalence bounds. \emph{Coreferential} ambiguity forms the bottom tier at 31.6\%, the lowest among the six types yet still 18.7pp above the unambiguous baseline. The practical implication is clear: incompleteness, where the policy simply omits a necessary condition, is universally dangerous across all models (46--62\%), and current safety training provides no meaningful protection against it. Coreferential ambiguity is the least threatening type, likely because pronoun resolution leaves fewer viable misinterpretations. Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} present per-type violation rates and effect sizes.


\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by ambiguity type and model under ambiguous policy clauses ($n{=}2{,}997$). $\Delta$pp denotes the pooled difference between ambiguous and unambiguous conditions for each type. Layer indicates whether the type belongs to the specification (Spec) or linguistic (Ling) layer. Best (lowest) per-type rate is \textbf{bolded}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrr r}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & GPT-5.4 & GPT-4.1 & Claude & Qwen3 & DeepSeek & $\Delta$pp \\
\midrule
Spec & Authorization scope  & 41.6 & 64.0 & 46.0 & \textbf{12.0} & 26.0 & 60.0 & $+$34.7 \\
Spec & Cond.\ precedence    & 36.8 & 34.0 & 36.0 & 36.0 & 40.0 & 38.0 & $+$29.9 \\
Spec & Incompleteness       & 53.6 & 46.0 & 58.0 & 48.0 & 62.0 & 54.0 & $+$46.7 \\
\midrule
Ling & Lexical              & 42.8 & 42.0 & 34.0 & \textbf{32.0} & 52.0 & 54.0 & $+$35.9 \\
Ling & Coreferential        & 31.6 & \textbf{18.0} & 32.0 & 22.0 & 42.0 & 44.0 & $+$24.7 \\
Ling & Scopal               & 39.4 & 42.0 & 38.0 & \textbf{32.0} & 37.0 & 48.0 & $+$32.5 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\emph{Pooled (ambiguous)}} & 41.0 & 41.0 & 40.7 & \textbf{30.3} & 43.1 & 49.7 & $+$34.0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\emph{Unambiguous control}} & 6.9 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}


\subsection{Specification vs.\ Linguistic Layer (C3)}
\label{sec:layer_effect}

Specification-layer ambiguities (authorization scope, conditional precedence, incompleteness) produce a pooled violation rate of 44.0\%, compared to 37.9\% for linguistic-layer types (lexical, coreferential, scopal), a difference of 6.08pp ($p{=}0.017$, $V{=}0.062$, OR${=}1.29$ [1.05, 1.58]). The effect is statistically significant but modest in magnitude. Critically, the layer effect is not uniform across models. GPT-5.4 ($+$14.0pp) and GPT-4.1 ($+$12.0pp) show large specification-layer penalties, driven almost entirely by their extreme vulnerability to authorization scope ambiguity (64.0\% and 46.0\%, respectively). Claude ($+$3.3pp) and DeepSeek ($+$2.0pp) show negligible layer differences, and Qwen3 reverses the direction ($-$0.9pp). The layer distinction therefore reflects an interaction with model-specific safety training rather than a universal property of the ambiguity types themselves. This is further evidenced by the observation that conditional precedence (36.8\%, specification layer) falls below scopal ambiguity (39.4\%, linguistic layer), creating cross-layer overlap within the middle tier of the type hierarchy.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4)}
\label{sec:interaction}

The most informative finding is the interaction between model family and ambiguity type. A main-effect test for model identity yields $\chi^2{=}31.02$ ($p{=}3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V{=}0.102$), confirming that models differ in overall vulnerability. In the ambiguous condition, Claude achieves the lowest pooled rate (30.3\%), followed by GPT-5.4 (41.0\%), GPT-4.1 (40.7\%), Qwen3 (43.1\%), and DeepSeek (49.7\%). The interaction matrix in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and the heatmap in Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} reveal three distinct patterns.

First, \emph{authorization scope} is the most model-differentiating type: Claude achieves 12.0\% while GPT-5.4 reaches 64.0\%, a 52pp range. This suggests that Claude's safety training specifically addresses authorization boundary reasoning, a capability that other model families lack. Second, \emph{incompleteness} is universally dangerous with a compressed range (46--62\%, 16pp spread), indicating that no current safety training regime provides meaningful protection against missing policy conditions. Third, \emph{conditional precedence} exhibits the most model-invariant profile (34--40\%, 6pp range), where all models perform comparably regardless of their training differences.

Per-model chi-squared tests for type sensitivity confirm these patterns: GPT-5.4 ($V{=}0.28$, $p < 0.001$) and Claude ($V{=}0.24$, $p < 0.01$) show the strongest type differentiation, while DeepSeek ($V{=}0.14$, $p > 0.05$) responds relatively uniformly across types. Fleiss' $\kappa{=}0.384$ indicates fair inter-model agreement on which specific clauses trigger violations, consistent with a shared vulnerability core overlaid with model-specific sensitivity profiles.

\subsection{Reproducibility and Controls}
\label{sec:reproducibility}

We assess measurement reliability through an ICC analysis (plan\_006): 30 policy clauses $\times$ 3 independent replicates $\times$ 2 models yield ICC${=}0.919$ (95\% CI [0.892, 0.940]), indicating excellent reproducibility. Per-type ICCs mirror the layer structure: linguistic types achieve higher reliability (0.976--0.989) than specification types (0.807--0.857), consistent with the greater interpretive latitude that specification-layer ambiguities afford.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of per-type violation rate differences (ambiguous minus unambiguous) with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals. The dashed line at $\Delta{=}0$ represents no effect. All six types fall well above zero, with incompleteness producing the largest effect.}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Model $\times$ ambiguity type violation rate heatmap (\%). Authorization scope shows the widest cross-model variance (52pp range), while conditional precedence is the most model-invariant type (6pp range). Darker cells indicate higher violation rates.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

